ly evaluated on its own task — cost ≈ same total as one full pass since each adapter is only evaluated on its own task; if the ensemble is evaluated over all tasks the cost is 8x). So: C1 (per-task adapter selection) cost ≈ 1 pass ≈ same as A/B. C2 (simple ensemble / voting across all adapters on all tasks) ≈ 8 passes. Estimate: A=1, B=1, C-selection=1, C-ensemble=8 → ~11 passes × ~3–5 GPU-hours = 33–55 GPU-hours on an A100 80GB; plus overhead, totals ~40–60 GPU-hours, fitting within a single day on 4 A100s. D (capacity control, e.g., a LoRA of equivalent parameter budget trained on a concatenation of CS training sets — a random initialization or multi-task single adapter) adds training cost; mark it as optional.\n\nSmoke test on CPU: use sshleifer/tiny-gpt2 or a small llama (hf-internal/tiny-random-llama) with 20 items per task to validate the pipeline. 224 CPUs available, CPU inference of a tiny model is fine.\n\nMcNemar + multiple-comparison correction: Holm–Bonferroni over 8 tests per comparison; bootstrap 10,000 resamples at the item level for macro-average CI; cluster bootstrap per task too? Keep it simple: item-level bootstrap within a task, and for the macro-average resample items within each task and then average.\n\nPre-registration of success criteria: significant if macro-average difference > 0 and the 95% bootstrap CI of (B−A) excludes 0, plus a minimum effect size of e.g. ≥1 point; declare the practical equivalence band up front.\n\nAnti-contamination measures: n-gram overlap between test items and the C4/Raven pretraining corpus (using 13-gram matching per the Llama-2 paper methodology), report exclusion sensitivity analysis.\n\nLet me also record the dataset sizes as estimates (well-known canonical test split numbers — should I label them as \"approximate, to be verified at runtime via hashes\").\n\nWrite the DESIGN.md file to /workspace, then call end_researcher_turn at stage=result including the full design in the answer.\n\nLet me run the resource